L-21,12,G13,"Bury St Edmunds household","Bury St Mary register (Suffolk RO Bury, FL 541/4) — three 1653-1656 Bury Gurney burials","Register-image pull to test the Bury apprenticeship hypothesis and household structure.",Y,"DEPRIORITIZED 2026-06-15 (user) — Bury is worked ground / known-known: the case file already eliminates the Bury John (son of Thomas, bp 1653) as the emigrant given his Bury BURIAL, and done-lead L-96 closed Bury as a resolved-negative for the John origin (existing corpus file sources/corpus_supplement/bury-st-edmunds-gurney-registers-1561-1800.md + validation). Do not spend further cycles unless a Bury freemen/apprentice 'John Gurney tailor' in-window turns up incidentally. [prior pass, for record:] a Bury St Edmunds Gurney FAMILY is in the printed St James register (Hervey/Suffolk Green Books transcript, clean-OCR in FTS): Bridget Gurney dau of WILLIAM bp Sept 1588 (ark 3:1:3QSQ-G9C5-3Z4R); 'Juda Gurney' buried 1634 at St James (ark 3:1:3QS7-89C5-3ZNK); John Gurney son of THOMAS 1653 (ark 3:1:3QS7-89C5-3ZFM) — extending the known 1653-56 Bury Gurney burials back to a William household by 1588. So a persistent Bury Gurney presence spans 1588-1656, the right town/era for the tailor-apprenticeship hypothesis (page also carries glover/maltster/tailour/weaver trades), though none of these is obviously the emigrant John. NEXT: collection-scoped Gurn* sweep of the full Bury St James + St Mary printed registers for an in-window John, and Bury freemen/apprentice records (point-3 tailor vector).",research/people/g13-john-gurney-fact-sheet.research.md
