L-138,45,Related,"Clement Gurney, gent — rare-forename Gurney (1622 bond)","NCC regd. will 1631-32, paleo packet 16a (ark 3:1:3Q9M-CSN8-YRB3); adjacent register opening page","A testator's will (NCC registered copy, 1631-32) names 'Clement my sonne' and 'Clement Gurney gent' bound in a 40-pound/20-pound bond dated 21 Nov 1622. Clement is a rare Gurney forename (high tracing value); the captured page is a continuation, so testator and parish are unidentified — read the adjacent opening page to name the testator and locate this gentleman Gurney.",Y,"WILL LOCATED 2026-06-15 — spans images 363-365 of DGS 008076514 (arks YTWS / YT9K / YRB3; 'Norwich. Wills 1632, 1631', NCC reg. vol.124-125). Testator is a NORFOLK YEOMAN, will dated 7 Charles I (1631/32), Diocese of Norwich; wife GRISSELL/Crissell; a son/legatee CLEMENT; further legatees John Powles & Thomas Rowles with bequests payable 'at the end of their apprentishood'; the testator holds the £40 bond in which 'Clement Gurney Gent' is bound to him, dated 21 Nov 1622, and gives it to 'Clement my sonne'. The testator's NAME + PARISH read as OCR salad at the will opening (image 363 foot) — needs paleography. THREE pages downloaded + staged as PALEOGRAPHY PACKET 20 (extends packet 16a). CRUX for the paleographer: is 'Clement my sonne' = 'Clement Gurney Gent' (i.e. testator himself a Gurney), or two different Clements? That decides whether this is a Gurney testator's will.",research/people/g13-john-gurney-fact-sheet.research.md
